WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.003
A customer reports a failed checkout at about 09:06, and quotes the reference 7f3a9c.

2
00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:13.879
The services write that request ID on every log line.

3
00:00:13.879 --> 00:00:15.806
So the story is in the logs.

4
00:00:15.806 --> 00:00:17.869
But where, and in which lines?

5
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.496
A log is a plain text file that a program adds to, one line per event.

6
00:00:23.496 --> 00:00:26.784
A time, a level, the request ID, and a message.

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.557
This one has eight lines.

8
00:00:28.557 --> 00:00:30.923
On a server, a log can be 2 gigabytes.

9
00:00:30.923 --> 00:00:34.721
cat would print it all, and it would scroll past for minutes.

10
00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:39.287
grep answers one question: which lines contain this text?

11
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:45.224
It reads its input one line at a time, and checks whether the pattern appears anywhere in the line.

12
00:00:45.433 --> 00:00:47.697
Lines that contain it are printed.

13
00:00:47.697 --> 00:00:49.095
The rest are skipped.

14
00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:52.077
Five lines.

15
00:00:52.077 --> 00:00:57.439
Like a highlighter dragged down a printout, then you cut out only the marked lines.

16
00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:02.690
Each option changes one part of that, and answers one question.

17
00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:03.336
How many?

18
00:01:03.336 --> 00:01:05.419
-c counts the matching lines.

19
00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:07.778
What is left without it?

20
00:01:07.778 --> 00:01:09.217
-v inverts the match.

21
00:01:09.217 --> 00:01:12.575
Now grep prints only the lines it skipped before.

22
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.461
Plain words only get you so far.

23
00:01:16.461 --> 00:01:21.103
Say you want every duration: 2ms, 34ms, 2900ms.

24
00:01:21.103 --> 00:01:24.264
No single word matches them all.

25
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:27.164
But they share a shape: digits, then ms.

26
00:01:27.164 --> 00:01:31.008
A regular expression is a pattern that describes a shape.

27
00:01:31.233 --> 00:01:34.566
Square brackets with 0 to 9 match any one digit.

28
00:01:34.566 --> 00:01:36.579
Plus means one or more of it.

29
00:01:36.579 --> 00:01:38.037
Then the letters m s.

30
00:01:38.667 --> 00:01:43.453
When grep reads a line, it tries the pattern at every position.

31
00:01:43.453 --> 00:01:47.328
Here, 09 is digits, but then comes a colon, not ms.

32
00:01:47.328 --> 00:01:47.860
No fit.

33
00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:52.070
At 200, the digits fit, but then comes a space.

34
00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:52.666
No fit.

35
00:01:52.666 --> 00:01:55.392
At 34ms, the whole pattern fits.

36
00:01:55.392 --> 00:01:56.840
The line matches.

37
00:01:57.067 --> 00:01:59.848
And -o prints only the part that matched.

38
00:01:59.848 --> 00:02:01.816
grep turns into an extractor.

39
00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:06.344
Plus belongs to extended regular expressions, so you add -E.

40
00:02:06.344 --> 00:02:09.577
In plain grep, plus is an ordinary character.

41
00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.426
The same trap catches the bar.

42
00:02:12.426 --> 00:02:17.883
grep with WARN bar ERROR, and no -E, looks for that exact text, bar included.

43
00:02:17.883 --> 00:02:19.088
It finds nothing.

44
00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:22.955
With -E, the bar means or, and both lines come back.

45
00:02:23.767 --> 00:02:26.922
Sometimes the question is about files, not lines.

46
00:02:26.922 --> 00:02:29.433
Which logs mention this request at all?

47
00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:35.177
find walks down through every folder under a starting point, and tests each file it meets.

48
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.268
Here the test is a name.

49
00:02:37.268 --> 00:02:40.380
Star dot log is a glob, so it is quoted.

50
00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.481
Each name that fits passes.

51
00:02:42.481 --> 00:02:43.960
notes.txt does not.

52
00:02:44.167 --> 00:02:45.569
It never opens a file.

53
00:02:45.569 --> 00:02:51.241
It only looks at what it knows about each one: the name, the type, the size, and the age.

54
00:02:51.467 --> 00:02:54.545
Then -exec hands the list of paths to grep.

55
00:02:54.767 --> 00:03:00.450
grep reads every line of them, and -l prints only the names of files that match.

56
00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:01.089
All four.

57
00:03:01.089 --> 00:03:03.576
Four services touched this request.

58
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.062
find picks files, by name, type, size or age.

59
00:03:08.062 --> 00:03:10.942
grep picks lines, by a word or a pattern.

60
00:03:10.942 --> 00:03:13.331
Its options answer your questions.

61
00:03:13.331 --> 00:03:15.017
And quote every pattern.
